# How to use React Router with Laravel?

When you attempt to serve a Single Page Application (SPA) built with React inside a Laravel backend, a specific architectural conflict arises immediately upon navigating away from the root path. The scenario is common: you define a route in `React Router` for `/profile`, but when the browser requests that URL, Laravel's server-side router returns a "Route not found" error. This happens because React Router only manages client-side navigation; it does not intercept HTTP requests sent directly to the web server.

To resolve this, you must understand that Laravel sees every request as a standard HTTP transaction, whereas React expects the entire application logic to reside on the client side after the initial load. The solution requires bridging the gap between the two environments by configuring Laravel to serve the `index.html` file for all requests that do not match defined backend routes.

### Configuring Laravel to Serve SPA Content

The core issue lies in how the web server handles requests. When you navigate to `/profile`, the React application attempts to render a component. However, since there is no corresponding route defined in your Laravel `web.php` or similar files for `/profile`, the framework treats it as a 404 error. You need to modify the route definition to catch these unmatched requests and return the entry point of your React app.

In most modern Laravel versions (6.2+), you can add a single catch-all route at the end of your `routes/web.php` file:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;

// Existing routes...
Route::get('/dashboard', [AppController::class, 'index'])->name('dashboard');

// Catch-all route for React Router paths
Route::any('{any}', function () {
    return view('welcome'); // This should render your React index.html
})->where({
    '_route' => ''
});
```

Alternatively, if you are using Laravel Mix or Vite to build your assets, ensure the `welcome` view is actually a Blade template that contains only the `<div id="root"></div>` container. If you have already compiled your React app into a static file (e.g., `public/build/index.html`), you can return that file directly:

```php
Route::get('/{any}', function () {
    if (!empty($_GET['?'])) {
        return back(); // Handle query string parameters if necessary
    }
    return view('app'); // Ensure 'app' points to your compiled SPA entry point
})->where('_route', '');
```

Note that in newer Laravel versions, simply returning `view('welcome')` might not be sufficient if the asset is pre-compiled. You may need to adjust the path to point directly to the built assets or ensure the Blade file correctly references the script tags for your React build.

### Handling History State and SEO

When using `browserHistory` in your React Router configuration, as shown in your snippet:

```javascript
render((
    <Router history={browserHistory}>
        <Route path="/" component={App}/>
        <Route path="/profile" component={Profile}/> 
    </Router>
), document.getElementById('root'));
```

The `browserHistory` library utilizes the HTML5 History API to manipulate the URL without reloading the page. This is essential for a seamless user experience, but it requires the server to behave differently than default routing. With `hashHistory`, the URL would include a `#` symbol (e.g., `/#/profile`), which Laravel can handle natively without special configuration because it matches the route based on the path before the hash.

By switching to `browserHistory`, you gain cleaner URLs that are better for SEO and sharing, but you lose the ability to rely on standard server routing logic. Every navigation event must be intercepted by your backend. This is why the catch-all route mentioned earlier is mandatory; without it, any deep link or bookmarked URL will break the application immediately.

### Asset Compilation Considerations

Before relying on the route configuration, ensure your React assets are properly compiled. If you are using `react-scripts`, the build process generates an `index.html` file. You must serve this specific file from Laravel. If you are integrating React into a larger Laravel project, it is often cleaner to keep the compilation separate or use a tool like `laravel-mix` to bundle your React entry point directly into the Laravel asset pipeline.

If you choose to compile assets manually, verify that the `public/js/app.js` and `public/css/app.css` files are correctly linked in your Blade template returned by the catch-all route. A common pitfall is returning a generic 404 page instead of the React entry point when the backend route isn't found, which results in a blank screen or a white flash before the error appears.

### Finalizing the Setup

Once the catch-all route is in place and your `index.html` is correctly served, navigate to `/profile` in your browser. The request hits Laravel, fails to find a matching backend route, falls through to the catch-all, and serves the React application. React Router then takes over, rendering the `Profile` component without a page reload.

This pattern ensures that your SPA functions seamlessly within the Laravel ecosystem while maintaining clean URLs and proper state management on the client side. For further guidance on advanced Laravel asset compilation or integrating specific build tools with your existing stack, you might find additional resources at https://laravelcompany.com.
